{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017MXrJJaNGsktWrX2STadkD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The result is 30801. This means:\n- Step 3 (30000 range) = cuMemMap with offset=0 succeeded, then cuMemMap with offset=page_size FAILED\n- Wait no, let me re-read the test function:\n\n```c\n// Map page 0 (offset=0)\nr = cuMemMap(va, page_size, 0, handle, 0);\nif (r != CUDA_SUCCESS) { ...; return 30000 + (int)r; }\n```\n\n\nActually, 30801 means the first cuMemMap call itself failed with CUDA_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED. That's strange because the existing code uses this same pattern successfully — the key difference is that my test allocates a handle for two pages while the existing code only allocates one page, so the handle size mismatch is likely causing the mapping to fail.\n\nIt looks like this GPU enforces a strict requirement: cuMemMap can only map the entire physical allocation, not a sub-range. That's a significant constraint — it means bulk allocations can't be partially mapped or defragmented at the page level. I need to design around this by only using full-allocation mappings and accepting that bulk allocations are immutable once mapped.\n\nFor defragmentation, I have a few options: either skip moving bulk-allocated regions entirely, unmap and remap the entire bulk allocation as a unit when needed, or break up the allocation into individual pages when it's freed. The second approach seems most workable — when defrag encounters pages from a bulk allocation, I'd check if all pages in that range are being remapped together, and if so, unmap the old VA range and remap the entire allocation at the new location. But if only part of the bulk allocation needs remapping, that becomes problematic.\n\nLooking at how defragmentation actually works, partial remaps do occur — a free region might be larger than what's needed, so only a portion gets taken for remapping. This means I'd need to unmap the entire bulk range, create individual-page allocations for the pages being remapped, remap those individually, and then re-map the remaining pages. That's getting quite complex, so I'm examining the code flow more carefully to see if there's a simpler path forward.\n\nThe defrag logic takes a slice from the start of a free region and puts any leftover back into the free pool. So for a 48 MiB bulk-allocated region where only 24 MiB is needed, the first 24 MiB gets remapped to new virtual addresses while the remaining 24 MiB stays put.\n\nBut here's the problem: all those pages share a single handle, and while I can unmap an aligned sub-range, I can't remap just that sub-range to a different virtual address without an offset parameter that `cuMemMap` doesn't support. So I'm reconsidering the whole approach — instead of trying to remap sub-ranges, what if I store the bulk allocation as a single entry in active_pages with the full size, then track allocations differently?\n\nActually, I'm overcomplicating this. Let me step back and try the simplest solution: just don't make bulk pages defragmentable at all.\n\nWhen creating new pages in the defragmentation process, I'll bulk-allocate them together and map the entire region at once. Then I'll mark those pages as non-defragmentable using a special sentinel value, and skip them during remapping. The tradeoff is that bulk-allocated free regions won't participate in defragmentation, which could cause the pool to fail on requests needing contiguous memory.\n\nBut wait—I need to think through whether bulk-allocated pages actually become free regions that need defragmentation in the first place. Let me trace a concrete scenario: if I allocate 48 MiB, then another 48 MiB, what happens when one gets freed?\n\nWhen the first allocation is freed, it creates a 48 MiB free region. If a later 192 MiB allocation needs defragmentation, I'd bulk-allocate 144 MiB of new pages and then remap the existing 48 MiB free region into the new contiguous space. The problem is that those 48 MiB pages all belong to the same bulk handle, so I need to unmap the entire bulk allocation before remapping individual pages into the new location.\n\nBut this approach breaks down if only part of a bulk region is free. Say I allocate 48 MiB, free it all, then allocate just 24 MiB from that freed space—now I have 24 MiB allocated and 24 MiB free within the same bulk handle. I can't simply remap the entire bulk region because part of it is still in use.\n\nThe real issue is that a bulk handle maps to a contiguous VA range, and I can't move sub-ranges independently. So I think the solution is to split bulk allocations during `free_internal`—when a bulk-allocated buffer is freed, immediately break it into individual pages instead of keeping it as one large contiguous block.\n\nThe approach would be to unmap each page from the bulk mapping, create individual handles for each page, and remap them separately. This is expensive since it creates N new handles during the free operation—about 51ms for 128 pages—but that's the same cost as the original allocation, so I'm just shifting the overhead rather than eliminating it.\n\nBut wait, the allocation happens at the start of the first segment while the free happens after the proof is complete. If the free isn't on the critical path, I still gain something. Looking at the actual benchmark timing though, both the initial 48 MiB allocation and its subsequent free are happening during setup before proving starts, so they're both on the critical path. The net savings would be zero.\n\nInstead of splitting pages during free, I could handle bulk pages directly in the defrag code. When encountering a page from a bulk allocation, I'd find all pages from that allocation and process them together...\n\nBut this breaks down if only part of the bulk range needs to be moved during defrag. I'm realizing this optimization is more intricate than I initially thought.\n\nSince offset-based cuMemMap isn't s