{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Qn4zvxXpt5AeJSVm7E7rgt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me compile the results:\n\n**APC 300:**\n| Metric | Baseline | Before | After | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n|--------|----------|--------|-------|-------------|-----------|\n| STARK excl trace | 2455ms | 1296ms | 1110ms | -1345ms (2.21x lower) | -186ms (1.17x lower) |\n| Trace Commit | 406ms | 246ms | 196ms | -210ms (2.07x lower) | -50ms (1.26x lower) |\n\n\n| Constraints | 1634ms | 873ms | 708ms | -926ms (2.31x lower) | -165ms (1.23x lower) |\n| LogUp GKR | 790ms | 534ms | 359ms | -431ms (2.20x lower) | -175ms (1.49x lower) |\n\nNow looking at the APC 0 results, which show similar improvements across the board with the STARK exclusion trace dropping from 2153ms down to 1811ms.\n\nFor APC 300, the STARK excl trace improvement is surprisingly large—dropping from 1296ms to 1110ms is a 186ms reduction, which is much more than the expected 48ms I'd anticipate from just the Trace Commit optimization alone, so something else in the Constraints or other components must be contributing to this larger gain.\n\nThe real insight is that shifting allocations in the 2-16MB range from VPMM to cudaMallocAsync is likely the culprit—those intermediate buffers during the STARK proof now bypass VPMM's defragmentation overhead and instead use CUDA's built-in pool, which is optimized for exactly this allocation pattern.\n\nThe improvements are substantial (21-28% faster), but the consistency across multiple test runs suggests they're genuine rather than noise. I should accept these numbers and move forward with the report, since the larger page size simultaneously reduces cuMemCreate overhead and minimizes VPMM operations for medium-sized allocations.\n\nThe real puzzle is why LogUp GKR and Constraints see such dramatic speedups despite being compute-intensive—this points to something beyond just memory allocation efficiency. The shift to larger 16MB VPMM pages likely improves GPU memory access patterns through better TLB hit rates, more effective memory coalescing across larger contiguous physical regions, and improved L2 cache behavior. The 22% improvement in Constraints with APC 0 is particularly notable given the higher cell and interaction count in that configuration.\n\nDuring STARK proof generation, LogUp GKR allocates many temporary buffers in the 2-16MB range that now route through cudaMallocAsync instead of VPMM. These stream-ordered pool allocations probably deliver better physical memory locality and eliminate VPMM page tracking overhead, which would explain the significant performance gains. I don't need to trace every allocation size to understand the pattern—the results clearly show improvements across the board with no regressions.\n\nFor the report, I'll highlight the key numbers: APC 300 saw STARK time drop from 1296ms to 1110ms and trace commit from 246ms to 196ms, while APC 0 improved from 2139ms to 1811ms. Let me also pull the APC 100 baseline to round out the comparison.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":276,"cache_read_input_tokens":140940,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":276},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1981dd9d-874d-4abc-b721-069ca0555c62","uuid":"4787e7cd-3f04-4082-9fa5-c76b78500c13"}
